    tldr: "A public academic hospital in Singapore, part of the SingHealth group. Its department for blood cancers is run jointly with the National Cancer Centre Singapore, and its urology centre treats cancers of the kidney, bladder and prostate.",
    summary: "Singapore General Hospital describes itself as a tertiary academic hospital that integrates clinical care with research and education. Its Department of Haematology, run jointly with the National Cancer Centre Singapore and established in 1985, covers benign and malignant blood disorders, including acute leukaemias, chronic lymphocytic and chronic myeloid leukaemia, lymphoma and multiple myeloma, with haematopoietic cell therapy and transplant, cancer survivorship care and clinical trials; as the haematology service of SingHealth it also serves Changi General Hospital, KK Women's and Children's Hospital and the National Cancer Centre. Its Urology Centre treats genitourinary cancers within a uro-oncology service. Newsweek and Statista's World's Best Specialized Hospitals 2026 list for oncology ranks it 177.",
    programs: ["Haematology with the National Cancer Centre Singapore (blood cancers, haematopoietic cell therapy and transplant)", "Uro-oncology (Urology Centre)"],
