    tldr: "Inha University's teaching hospital in Incheon, South Korea. Its comprehensive cancer centre treats blood cancers and solid tumours in adults and children, and runs a blood and marrow transplant programme.",
    summary: "인하대학교병원 (Inha University Hospital) says it opened in May 1996, and it also runs the medical centre at Incheon International Airport. Its Comprehensive Cancer Center brings together medical oncology, paediatric oncology, radiotherapy, surgery, pathology, radiology and nuclear medicine, and lists the cancers it treats, from leukaemia, lymphoma and myeloma through gastric, lung, breast, gynaecological, liver, colorectal, oesophageal, pancreatic and bile duct cancers to brain tumours, sarcomas, gestational trophoblastic disease and childhood cancers. The centre names its blood and marrow transplant programme as a strength, offers clinical trials, a cancer education programme for patients and families, and a home care programme for people at the end of life. The hospital also lists separate head and neck cancer, lung cancer, breast and thyroid surgery, robotic surgery and palliative care centres. Newsweek and Statista's World's Best Specialized Hospitals 2026 list for oncology ranks it 242.",
    programs: ["Comprehensive Cancer Center", "Blood and marrow transplant programme", "Head and neck cancer centre", "Lung cancer centre", "Palliative care centre", "Cancer education programme", "Home care programme for people with advanced cancer"],
